\section{Plug-in Standard Error Validation}
\label{app:bootstrap_se}

A natural concern is whether the plug-in standard error adequately captures estimation uncertainty, particularly the additional variance introduced by confusion matrix estimation.
We verify this for the difference-in-means (DIM) estimator via Monte Carlo simulation ($K{=}2$, $n_{\text{expert}} \in \{50, 100, 250, 500\}$, 50 replications per budget level).
At each budget, we compute the plug-in 95\% confidence interval and record empirical coverage.
Across all expert budgets, the plug-in SE yields coverage $\geq 0.96$ (0.970, 0.960, 0.970, 0.970 for $n_{\text{expert}} = 50, 100, 250, 500$), confirming that the analytic variance estimate is well-calibrated for the DIM estimator.
This self-calibration arises because the same noise in $\hat{M}^{-1}$ that inflates point-estimate variance also inflates the plug-in SE, producing conservative intervals that maintain nominal coverage.

\section{Robustness to Correlated Misclassification and Treatment Effect Heterogeneity}
\label{app:a5_violation}

Our main analysis assumes that subgroup misclassification rates $\pi_s$ and subgroup CATE magnitudes $\Delta\tau_s$ are independent (Assumption A5).
In practice, both may depend on text features that define subgroups, introducing correlation.
We test robustness under two configurations ($K{=}2$, $N{=}5{,}000$, $n_{\text{expert}} \in \{250, 500\}$, 50 MC replications):
\emph{aligned} (positive correlation: the high-error subgroup has the larger treatment effect, $\pi = [0.05, 0.25]$, $\Delta\tau = [0.30, 0.70]$) and
\emph{adversarial} (negative correlation: the high-error subgroup has the smaller treatment effect, same $\pi$, $\Delta\tau = [0.70, 0.30]$).

In the aligned configuration, the $\delta_s \times \Delta\tau_s$ product is maximized in the high-error subgroup, amplifying naive bias: naive coverage on S1 drops to 0.25 at $n_{\text{expert}}{=}250$.
Global correction improves but remains miscalibrated (S1 coverage 0.82), because the pooled confusion matrix cannot account for subgroup-specific error rates.
EC-HTE maintains coverage at 0.95 across both subgroups.
In the adversarial configuration, global correction overcorrects the low-error subgroup (S0 coverage 0.74), while EC-HTE again maintains 0.94--0.96 coverage.
At $n_{\text{expert}}{=}500$, all EC-HTE coverages remain within $[0.93, 0.99]$ under both configurations.
These results confirm that EC-HTE's subgroup-specific correction is robust to A5 violations: coverage remains near-nominal regardless of the correlation structure between misclassification rates and treatment effect heterogeneity.

